# Copy this file to .env.local for local development.
#
# Do NOT put secrets in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable: Next.js inlines them into the
# browser bundle, where anyone can read them.

# Base URL of the PMT backend API.
#
# Port 5050, which is the BACKEND. The dashboard itself runs on 3000. This file
# used to point at the frontend's own port, so a fresh clone had the dashboard
# calling itself and every request 404'd against Next's router.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL="http://localhost:5050"

# Optional. Set to "off" to disable the optimistic session guard in `proxy.ts`.
#
# That guard redirects a visitor with no session cookie away from /dashboard
# before anything renders, which removes the flash of a dashboard that then
# 401s. It can only see the cookie if the browser sends it to BOTH hosts, which
# is true in development (cookies ignore the port, so :5050 and :3000 are one
# host) and true in production when the API and the dashboard share a
# registrable domain.
#
# If a deployment ever puts them on unrelated domains, the guard would see no
# cookie for a valid session and redirect every signed-in user to sign in. Turn
# it off there. Nothing is lost: the API is the actual control, and the
# dashboard still validates the session on load.
#
# Server-side only, deliberately: NEXT_PUBLIC_ would inline it into the browser
# bundle, and this is not a decision a browser takes part in.
# SESSION_GUARD="off"
